# rlnell.msg - Nell Harrow, madam of the Slag (Redding)
# Plain ASCII only: the English Fallout 2 fonts have no umlauts or typographic quotes.

# Report and payout
{100}{}{The shift siren went three times today. That's three rushes, and three times the girls washed the dust off the men and the gold off the counter. Here's the week.}
{121}{}{. Weighed twice. Gold dust sticks to fingers in this town, but not to mine.}
{122}{}{Nothing. Ascorti's man came before you did.}

# Prices and share
{130}{}{The miners get paid in dust and spend it on Jet before they spend it on us. Cheap fills the sheds. Dear keeps out the ones who'd trade a girl for a fix.}
{140}{}{Our people keep }
{141}{}{ percent of what they bring in. In the camp, that's more than a digger gets for a shift. It's still not much.}
{145}{}{A quarter. (She looks at her hands.) Like the mines. Take the gold, leave the slag.}
{146}{}{A third. Same as a digger's share. They'll call it fair, and they'll mean it about half.}
{147}{}{Almost half? (She stares at you.) Nobody in Redding has ever given a woman half of anything. They'll think it's a trick. Then they'll work like it isn't.}

# Morale
{150}{}{They sing when the siren goes. I haven't heard singing in the camp since the Jet came.}
{151}{}{They're tired. Everybody in Redding is tired. That's not a complaint, that's the weather.}
{152}{}{They're counting the days. Not until payday. Until they can leave.}
{153}{}{One of them is gone. Took a wagon to the mines. Anything's better than here, she said. She was wrong, but she said it.}

# Crisis: the drug
{160}{}{One of ours is on Jet. In Redding that's like saying the sun came up. But she's selling her share of the dust to pay for it, and soon she'll sell something worse.}
{165}{}{(Nell watches you for a long time.) My husband screamed like that for two days before he died. She's asleep now. Thank you.}
{166}{}{There's a man in the camp who went dry. He'll sit with her. It costs, but it works sometimes.}
{167}{}{(Her face goes still.) That's how the Mordinos keep the miners. Now it's how we keep the girls. I'll do it. Don't ask me to like you for it.}
{168}{}{Out, then. She'll be at the Jet shacks by nightfall. They all end up at the Jet shacks.}

# Look
{200}{}{A woman with red, cracked hands and a laundress's shoulders.}
{201}{}{Nell Harrow. She ran the camp laundry until Jet took her husband. Now she runs the Slag.}

# Construction and staff (her voice)
{460}{}{What do we build? First something that keeps the dust out. Then something that keeps the men in line.}
{463}{}{Something you only get in Redding.}
{483}{}{Nobody's looking for new faces.}
{484}{}{Our recruiter waits at the pay window. The widows come. There are always widows in Redding.}
{485}{}{Our recruiter buys up Jet debts. They come to pay them off. Most of them never do.}
{490}{}{I know every widow in the camp. I'll tell them there's a place with a lock on the inside.}
{491}{}{(Nell says nothing for a while.) The Mordinos did it to the miners with Jet. Now we do it with debts. I'll keep the books. I won't keep quiet about it.}

# First meeting
{800}{}{(A woman with red, cracked hands is scrubbing the counter as if she could get the gold dust out of the grain.) Nell Harrow. Pruett said you'd need someone who knows the camp. I know the camp. I know which foreman beats his wife and which one pays in fake dust. Rules: no Jet on the floor, no credit, and every girl keeps her own key.}
{801}{}{Show me how the house is doing.}

# The Malamute Saloon (Phase 3, 4.2)
{850}{}{About the Malamute.}
{851}{}{We're undercutting the Malamute. }
{852}{}{ week(s) so far. The girls hate the prices. The Malamute hates them more.}
{853}{}{The Malamute in Downtown keeps girls upstairs. Cheaper than us, and meaner. As long as they're open, the miners who don't want Jet go there first. We could buy in, starve them out, spoil their whiskey or buy the whole place.}
{854}{}{[Barter] Buy a share of their upstairs business. ($1500)}
{856}{}{Undercut them. Rock-bottom prices until they give up.}
{858}{}{[Sneak] Spoil their whiskey. Let the miners get sick at the Malamute.}
{860}{}{Buy them out. The rooms upstairs become ours. ($3000)}
{862}{}{Done. They keep their girls, we keep a share, and nobody fights over the same miners. Sixty a week, give or take.}
{863}{}{Rock-bottom, then. The girls will work twice as hard for half the dust. Four weeks, and the Malamute will close its upstairs. If we can last that long.}
{864}{}{(Three nights later half of Downtown is sick, and every one of them was drinking at the Malamute. Their upstairs closes within the week.)}
{865}{}{(Somebody saw you at the Malamute's back door. Sheriff Marion knows who poisoned the whiskey, and he isn't the kind of man who forgets.)}
{866}{}{Done. Two more rooms, and the girls from upstairs, if they want to stay. Most of them do. They've seen worse bosses.}

# Kitty's Claw (Phase 4, 3.6)
{870}{}{About Kitty's Claw.}
{874}{}{Kitty's Claw opened behind the Last Gasp. The Wrights stand at her door, and she pays our girls better than we do. She won't stop until one of us closes.}
{875}{}{[Speech] Make peace with Kitty. Whatever it costs. ($2000)}
{876}{}{Buy the Claw out from under her. Redding knows us better than her.}
{877}{}{Close the Claw. Any way it takes.}
{878}{}{(Kitty counts the money, pours two glasses and doesn't drink hers.) She says you're forgiven. She says it the way people say it in New Reno.}
{879}{}{(The Claw's landlord is a Redding man, and Redding listens to us now. Kitty leaves for good. Her girls stay, and so do two of her rooms.)}
{880}{}{(The Claw burns one night with nobody inside. Kitty leaves Redding. The Wrights don't forget whose men carried the gas.)}

